  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01862v1</id>
    <title>Interoperability Assessment of Fronthaul and E2 Interfaces for Multi-Vendor Open RAN Systems</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T12:54:11Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01862v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.01862v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Open Radio Access Networks (RANs) aim to transform the existing wireless network architectures by adopting open interfaces, disaggregating and virtualising RAN components, and enabling dynamic control of the system via data-driven methods. To accelerate the development of interoperable RAN solutions, rigorous assessment of Open Fronthaul and E2 interfaces based on O-RAN Alliance Interoperability Testing (IOT) specifications plays a key role as the integration of these interfaces seems to be very challenging, e.g. due to the need for alignment of Fronthaul IOT profiles and unforeseen challenges for E2 interface integration as Near-Real-Time RAN Intelligent Controller (Near-RT RIC) is relatively a new component introduced within the RAN architecture. In this paper, selected parts of the latest findings and lessons learned from interoperability assessment of Open Fronthaul and E2 interfaces for 13 multi-vendor Open RAN systems deployed in SmartRAN Open Network Interoperability Centre (SONIC) Labs are presented.</summary>
    <category term="cs.NI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="eess.SP" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T12:54:11Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Proceedings of European Wireless 2025, Sophia Antipolis, France, pp. 91-96. Published by VDE VERLAG</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.NI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Farhad Mehran</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Charles Turyagyenda</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Dritan Kaleshi</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00635v1</id>
    <title>From Confounding to Counterfactuals: A Cross-Layer Causal Model for RAN Scheduling</title>
    <updated>2026-10-31T05:01:51Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00635v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.00635v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Data-driven control in radio access networks (RANs) selects resource allocations using associations between control actions and key performance indicators (KPIs) learned from network telemetry. Because the shared radio environment influences both the scheduler's decisions and the resulting performance, a bias known as confounding, such associations overstate the effect of an allocation and misdirect resources toward user equipment (UEs) that cannot benefit. Moreover, individual UEs respond heterogeneously to the same decision, so even a corrected average effect does not identify which UEs gain from additional resources. While causal inference has recently been applied to RANs, existing approaches do not model the cross-layer mechanisms that connect an allocation to its outcome. In this paper, a hierarchical structural causal model (H-SCM) spanning the PHY, MAC, and network layers is proposed that separates cell-load confounding from UE-specific response heterogeneity. In particular, a counterfactual scheduling problem is formulated in which physical resource block (PRB) allocations are selected using per-UE counterfactual goodput rather than observational conditional averages. This problem is solved via a pipeline combining constrained causal discovery, structural-equation fitting, and abduction-action-prediction. Simulations demonstrate that naive observational estimation overstates the full-PRB response by 73.3%, whereas adjustment for cell-load confounding reduces the relative error to 9.8%. Held-out interventions are predicted with a coefficient of determination of 0.988. Counterfactual scheduling achieves a mean relative loss in total cell goodput below 0.5% against the ground-truth optimum, compared with 2.0-2.9% for a neural-network baseline.</summary>
    <category term="cs.NI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="eess.SP" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-31T05:01:51Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>6 pages, 4 figures, 1 table. Submitted to IEEE ICC 2027</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.NI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Artin Elhamirad</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Christo Kurisummoottil Thomas</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ali Tajer</name>
    </author>
