  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40744v1</id>
    <title>CLIC: Closed-Loop Intent Consistency for Securing LLM-Driven Control in Open RAN</title>
    <updated>2026-10-29T15:42:29Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40744v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40744v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>With the progressive capabilities of large language models (LLMs), researchers are finding ways to integrate them into the control loops of Open Radio Access Network (O-RAN) to enable slicing and resource management through user intents. However, integration of LLMs in the O-RAN control loop via the RAN Intelligent Controller (RIC) makes the system vulnerable to \textit{runtime prompt injection threats}, which manipulate control decisions without violating interface authentication, and \textit{residual virtual prompt injection (VPI)} attacks that plant trigger mechanisms to silently steer the behaviour during fine-tuning. Existing studies address only the runtime threat and do not enforce consistency across the full control path, i.e. from the certified A1 intent, through the multi-step LLM trajectory, to the final E2 action. We propose a closed-loop intent-consistency layer (CLIC) that jointly mitigates both threats. The proposed CLIC defines a formal consistency score using a small quantized language model, which is deployed as a Non-RT rApp for model-integrity certification along with a Near-RT xApp for runtime enforcement. We implement CLIC on the OpenAI Cellular (OAIC) platform and evaluate it under domain-adapted JailbreakBench, AdvBench, and VPI workloads. The experimental results show that the proposed CLIC reduces the attack success rate from 0.92 to 0.29 and residual VPI attacks from 0.37 to 0.04, while improving isolation and fairness at slightly higher latency costs, i.e. approximately 27 ms.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.NI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-29T15:42:29Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>6 pages, 1 figure, 3 tables</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Sunder Ali Khowaja</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kapal Dev</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Joongheon Kim</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Angelos Antonopoulos</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Nikolaos Bartzoudis</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32257v1</id>
    <title>When Simple Is Enough: Resource-Aware Heuristic versus DDQN for O-RAN Mobility Robustness</title>
    <updated>2026-10-23T07:13:57Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32257v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32257v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Open Radio Access Network (O-RAN) enables mobility-management xApps, but a learned controller is not necessarily the most effective or efficient handover policy. This paper compares standard Event A3, a resource- and mobility-aware heuristic, and a Double Deep Q-Network (DDQN) through a common xApp-oriented interface and paired system-level simulation. The heuristic combines radio quality, target-cell resources, UE mobility, and service class, with weights optimized offline using NSGA-II, while DDQN-Multi uses temporal radio and resource observations. All controllers are evaluated on identical UE trajectories, traffic assignments, and ray-traced radio inputs under Base, High-Mobility, and High-UE scenarios. Performance is assessed using service, handover, resource, energy, and fairness KPIs, complemented by CRITIC--TOPSIS ranking and complexity analysis. Results show that the heuristic achieves the lowest outage in all three scenarios and ranks first in the CRITIC--TOPSIS comparison, while retaining linear online complexity and interpretable decisions. DDQN-Multi improves target feasibility under congestion but requires offline training and produces more handovers and ping-pong events. The results indicate that lightweight resource-aware control can provide a favourable performance-complexity trade-off relative to DDQN under the considered scenarios.</summary>
    <category term="cs.NI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-23T07:13:57Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>accepted for 2026 IEEE Globecom Workshops (GC Wkshps): Workshop on Edge Intelligence meets AI-Native RAN: Theory, Algorithm and Implementation (GC Wkshps 2026-EdgeIntel-AINativeRAN)</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.NI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Ali Deylamy</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Nima Afraz</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
